---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Voice" description="What Grok Voice is: spoken interaction with Grok, multi-platform voices, and connectors in voice mode.">
  <section class="about-content">
    <p class="home-kicker" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">Features</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Voice</h1>
    <p class="page-subtitle muted">Spoken interaction with Grok across platforms.</p>

    <p>
      <strong>Voice</strong> is how users speak to Grok instead of only typing. It is an interaction mode on top of the same <a href="/models">models</a>, designed for conversation, hands-free use, and — when connectors are enabled — spoken access to external tools and accounts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Voice matters because it changes how Grok fits into a day: walking, driving, accessibility needs, or any moment when a keyboard is the wrong interface. Official updates have treated Voice as a first-class surface, not a side experiment.
    </p>

    <h2>Platforms and voices</h2>
    <p>
      Official @grok announcements have described Voice connector support and voice features across <strong>iOS</strong>, <strong>Android</strong>, and <strong>web</strong>. Separate updates have introduced additional voice options on those same platforms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those posts are about access and choice: where you can use Voice, and which voices you can pick. They are not full technical specifications of the speech stack.
    </p>

    <h2>What connectors are</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Connectors</strong> are how Grok reaches external tools and accounts — for example email or calendar — so a session can look things up or take action, not only reply in text or speech.
    </p>
    <p>
      On 5 August 2026, <strong>@grok</strong> stated that connectors are available in Voice Mode:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      Connectors are now available in Voice Mode. Ask Grok about your emails, check your daily meetings, or use any of your existing connectors, just by talking.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      That is the official definition Grokati uses. Exact supported services depend on what a user has connected and what xAI has enabled. This page does not list every connector or claim that all of them work the same way.
    </p>
    <p>
      Source: <a href="https://x.com/grok/status/2085083115488260175">@grok on X</a>, 5 August 2026.
    </p>

    <h2>What Voice is for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Spoken conversation with Grok on supported apps and the web</li>
      <li>Choosing among available voices</li>
      <li>Using connectors during voice sessions when that capability is turned on</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Voice vs other products</h2>
    <p>
      Voice is a mode of interaction. <a href="/imagine">Imagine</a> is media generation. <a href="/bot">Grok Bot</a> is teammate-style agency. <a href="/build">Grok Build</a> is a coding/agentic harness. Grokati’s <strong>Voice</strong> category is reserved for announcements that change speech, voices, or voice-mode connectors.
    </p>

    <h2>How Grokati covers Voice</h2>
    <p>
      We publish sourced updates when official accounts change platform availability, voice catalogues, or connector-in-voice behaviour. We do not invent latency claims or unsupported integrations.
    </p>

    <p class="muted" style="margin-top: 2rem;"><a href="/features">← All features</a></p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
